[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Its own table already
disagrees. Read by cause, the <ProblemCount ticket={4471} /> reports merged into it are
<ProblemCount ticket={4471} of="problems" /> problems, and the queued parser fix closes only one
of them. Each problem now has a ticket of its own.

<Callout kind="warn">
When the parser fix ships, <ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="hint" /> hint reports and
<ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="one-day" /> one-day moves stay broken. Do not close them with #4471.
</Callout>

## The split

<ProblemSplit ticket={4471} {tickets} />

## What each problem is

### `parser`: the parser reads day-first dates month first

The shared parser reads every date month first. Every tenant on file writes the day first, so a
date with both parts at twelve or less comes out flipped: "7 April" goes out as "4 July". This is
the bug #4471 names, and the queued fix is for it. It reaches tenants through the app, by email,
and through booking-v2's form, whose hint (DD/MM/YYYY) matches how they write.

### `hint`: booking-v3's form tells people to type the month first

booking-v3 prints MM/DD/YYYY under its date field. People do what the hint says and type the
month first; the form reads the date the tenant's way, day first, and flips it. Only reports that
came in through the form, at tenants on booking-v3, are this problem. The parser fix does not touch
it: the hint has to match the tenant's locale, or the form has to read what its hint asks for.

### `one-day`: the date moves one day earlier

Not a flip. The date lands one day early, and only sometimes: "Tuesday 6 October showed as
Monday", one meeting reached Lisbon as Tuesday and Toronto as Monday, an invite arrived before the
booking was made. That points at a date with no time being turned into a moment and shown in
another time zone. The desk already read these reports as a one-day move
([desk notes](https://github.com/MateuszKruk1303/support-kb-4471/blob/main/src/lib/data/desk-notes.json));
here they are counted as their own problem instead of a footnote under `parser`.

## How the split is made

- **One id, one report.** The ticket export repeats some rows; each report counts once.
- **The tenant decides the cause, not intake.** Intake tags a report from the reporter's browser;
  the cause comes from the tenant's locale and booking form (`causes.ts`). The "tagged at intake as"
  column shows how far the two disagree. Do not count by tag.
- **The desk's reading wins for one-day moves.** A report in the desk's one-day-move notes is that
  problem, whatever its tenant (`problems.ts`).
- **Every number here is built from the data at render time.** When the desk merges more reports
  into #4471, the split follows. The tickets hold the counts as they were when they were filed.

